editorialMd:
Build bestRewards

Each row of `drawers` is a booking: [start, end, reward]. It uses time from start up to, but not including, end. A booking may start exactly when another one ends. Sort the bookings by end time, then start time, then reward, all from smallest to largest. Look at the first booking, then the first two bookings, and so on. For each of these groups, choose bookings that do not overlap and give the largest total reward. Record that reward. You may choose no bookings for a reward of 0. With no bookings, `bestRewards` is empty.

Return the answer

Complete `paidReservationsProofStrip` so it returns `bestRewards`. Return it, do not print it.

Why this works

Each value in `bestRewards` follows task 1. The later tasks use only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n log n); extra memory, including the helper list, O(n). n is the input length. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

javascript
function paidReservationsProofStrip(drawers) {
  const a=drawers; let out=[];
  const rows=a.map(r=>[...r]).sort((x,y)=>x[1]-y[1]||x[0]-y[0]||x[2]-y[2]),dp=[0];for(let i=0;i<rows.length;i++){let l=0,r=i;while(l<r){const m=(l+r)>>1;if(rows[m][1]<=rows[i][0])l=m+1;else r=m;}dp.push(Math.max(dp[i],dp[l]+rows[i][2]));}out=dp.slice(1);
  return out;
}


